## gen_tcp

The `gen_tcp` module provides functions for communicating over TCP/IP protocol sockets. ([gen_tcp](https://www.erlang.org/doc/apps/kernel/gen_tcp.html))

## Echo server

Docs: [Echo server](https://elixir.hexdocs.pm/task-and-gen-tcp.html#echo-server)

A TCP server performs the following steps:

1. Listens to a port until the port is available and it gets hold of the socket
2. Waits for a client connection on that port and accepts it
3. Reads the client request and writes a response back

```elixir
defmodule KV.ServerOne do
  require Logger

  def accept(port) do
    {:ok, socket} =
      :gen_tcp.listen(port, [:binary, packet: :line, active: false, reuseaddr: true])
    Logger.info("Accepting connections on port #{port}")
    loop_acceptor(socket)
  end

  defp loop_acceptor(socket) do
    {:ok, client} = :gen_tcp.accept(socket)
    serve(client)
    loop_acceptor(socket)
  end

  defp serve(socket) do
    socket
    |> read_line()
    |> write_line(socket)

    serve(socket)
  end

  defp read_line(socket) do
    {:ok, data} = :gen_tcp.recv(socket, 0)
    data
  end

  defp write_line(line, socket) do
    :gen_tcp.send(socket, line)
  end
end
```

```elixir
# Start the server (commented out because it's interfering with later code)
# KV.ServerOne.accept(4041)
```

Connect to the server with a telnet client. E.g.:

```text
❯ telnet 127.0.0.1 4041
Trying 127.0.0.1...
Connected to 127.0.0.1.
Escape character is '^]'.
hello
hello
foo
foo
this is a test
this is a test
^]
telnet> q
Connection closed.
```

Note that closing the client currently generates the error:

```text
** (MatchError) no match of right hand side value:

    {:error, :closed}

    #cell:45ybwvp557oaut5m:26: KV.Server.read_line/1
    # ...
```

The error is because `:gen_tcp.recv/2` only matches `{:ok, data}`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
defp read_line(socket) do
  {:ok, data} = :gen_tcp.recv(socket, 0)
  data
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

### Handling TCP acceptor crashes

This is an attempt to implment some of what in [https://elixir.hexdocs.pm/task-and-gen-tcp.html](https://elixir.hexdocs.pm/task-and-gen-tcp.html) and [https://elixir.hexdocs.pm/docs-tests-and-with.html](https://elixir.hexdocs.pm/docs-tests-and-with.html) in a Livebook.

With the `ServerOne` code, the server won't be restarted if it dies - there's no supervisor process.

At a high level, what's going on in the `Scratch` and `Scratch.Server` modules below:

* `Scratch` calls `use Application` and implements a `start/2` function, that calls
* `Supervisor.start_link` on a list of child processes
* the first child process creates a `Task.Supervisor` named `Scratch.ServerSupervisor`
* the next child process is defined by calling `Supervisor.child_spec/2`
* it turns `Scratch.Server.accept(4040)` into a supervised task

```elixir
  Supervisor.child_spec({Task, fn -> Scratch.Server.accept(4040) end}, restart: :permanent)
```

```elixir
defmodule Scratch do
  use Application

  @impl true
  def start(_type, _args) do
    children = [
      {Task.Supervisor, name: Scratch.ServerSupervisor},
      Supervisor.child_spec({Task, fn -> Scratch.Server.accept(4040) end}, restart: :permanent)
    ]

    Supervisor.start_link(children, strategy: :one_for_one)
  end
end
```

`Scratch.Server.loop_acceptor/1` allows for simultaneous connections by having one process work as an acceptor (the process that accepts requests). `Task.Supervisor` is then used to serve each request. `:gen_tcp.controlling_process(client, pid)` makes the child process the "controlling process" of the `client` socket. Without this, the acceptor sould bring down all the clients if it crashed, because sockets would be tied to the process that accepted them.

`Scratch.Server.read_line` now handles error responses - it calls `exit\1` on error.

```elixir
defmodule Scratch.Server do
  require Logger

  def accept(port) do
    {:ok, socket} =
      :gen_tcp.listen(port, [:binary, packet: :line, active: false, reuseaddr: true])

    Logger.info("Accepting connections on port #{port}")
    loop_acceptor(socket)
  end

  defp loop_acceptor(socket) do
    {:ok, client} = :gen_tcp.accept(socket)
    {:ok, pid} = Task.Supervisor.start_child(Scratch.ServerSupervisor, fn -> serve(client) end)
    :ok = :gen_tcp.controlling_process(client, pid)
    loop_acceptor(socket)
  end

  defp serve(socket) do
    socket
    |> read_line()
    |> write_line(socket)

    serve(socket)
  end

  defp read_line(socket) do
    case :gen_tcp.recv(socket, 0) do
      {:ok, data} -> data
      {:error, :closed} -> exit(:shutdown) 
      {:error, reason} -> exit(reason)
    end
  end

  defp write_line(line, socket) do
    :gen_tcp.send(socket, line)
  end
end
```

```elixir
# In an actual mix app you'd call:
# Application.start(:scratch)

# possible (?) workaround for a Livebook:
# stop with Kino.terminate_child(supervisor)
# docs: https://kino.hexdocs.pm/Kino.html#start_child/1

{:ok, supervisor} = 
  Kino.start_child(%{
    id: Scratch,
    start: {Scratch, :start, [:normal, []]},
    type: :supervisor
  })
```

```elixir
# might be useful if things go wrong (?)
Kino.terminate_child(supervisor)
```

